00:04:46.320 --> 00:05:02.680
<v Ben Calhoun>And the letter Emily wrote was about a story she'd heard from a farmer. The farmer who told her this is apparently a person of some standing in the pork industry. And admittedly, I don't know the first thing about the pork industry, but he's in charge of a pork-producing operation that spans several states.
<v Ben Calhoun>艾米丽写的信是关于她从一位农场主那里听到的故事。告诉她这个故事的农场主显然在猪肉行业里颇有地位。平心而论，我并不懂猪肉行业，但他负责的猪肉生产业务跨越了好几个州。

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:21.010
<v Ben Calhoun>The story he told Emily went something like this, a while ago, he was visiting a pork processing plant in Oklahoma. He's walking through it with a friend, a guy who managed the plant, actually. And at some point, he saw boxes stacked on the floor, labeled "artificial calamari."
<v Ben Calhoun>他告诉艾米丽的故事大致是这样的：不久前，他参观了俄克拉荷马州的一家猪肉加工厂。他和朋友一起走进去，那是厂里的负责人。在某个点，他看到地上堆着标有“人造鱿鱼”字样的箱子。

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:39.940
<v Ben Calhoun>He stood there, wondering for a second. And then he asked his friend, what's artificial calamari? Bung, his friend replied. It's hog rectum, rectum that would be sliced into rings, deep fried, and boom, there you have it.
<v Ben Calhoun>他站在那里疑惑了一秒。然后他问朋友，什么是人造鱿鱼？“猪直肠，”他的朋友回答。“把它切成圆圈，油炸，砰，你就得到了。”

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:58.400
<v Ben Calhoun>OK, if I can, let me just narrate for you what this would mean. It would mean that in restaurants everywhere, right this second, people are squeezing lemon wedges over crispy, golden rings, and dipping the rings into marinara sauce, and they're eating hog rectum.
<v Ben Calhoun>好吧，如果可以的话，让我为你叙述这意味着什么。这意味着在各地的餐馆里，就在这一秒，人们正在把柠檬挤在金黄酥脆的圆圈上，把它们蘸进番茄酱里，而他们吃的是猪直肠。

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:14.630
<v Ben Calhoun>Now, they're chewing, satisfied, and deeply clueless. It's payback. It's payback for our blissful ignorance about where our food comes from and how it gets to us. It's amazing. And it's perfect.
<v Ben Calhoun>现在，他们正在咀嚼，感到满足，却对此一无所知。这是报应。这是我们对于食物来源及其加工过程的无知所带来的报应。这太惊人了，也太完美了。

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:39.950
<v Ben Calhoun>But it also seems like it couldn't possibly be true. So I called up the farmer to talk to him personally. I wanted to hear it firsthand. And the farmer confirmed the story-- the entire thing, the boxes, the bung. But when I asked him to go on the record, to tape an interview, and gave his name here, on the radio, he very politely declined, which seems suspicious, right?
<v Ben Calhoun>但这也听起来不太可能是真的。所以我打电话给那位农场主想亲自聊聊。我想亲耳听听。那位农场主证实了这个故事——整件事，箱子，直肠。但当我请他正式录音，把名字放在电台上时，他很礼貌地拒绝了，这看起来很可疑，对吧？

00:06:39.950 --> 00:07:00.820
<v Ben Calhoun>When I asked him why, he said he'd spoken with his girlfriend about it, and she suggested that he should think about the words that he wanted to come up when somebody Googled his name. This was all fine, though, because he referred me to the real expert, the guy who gave him the tour of the hog processing plant. And that guy, he agreed to talk.
<v Ben Calhoun>当我问他为什么时，他说他跟女朋友商量过，她建议他想想当别人在谷歌上搜索他的名字时，他想展现什么样的词条。不过这没关系，因为他把我介绍给了真正的专家，就是带他参观猪肉加工厂的那个人。那个人同意谈谈。

