00:08:42.320 --> 00:09:04.110
<v Ben Calhoun>OK, just to give a little better picture, a pork bung-- and "bung" is the actual industry term for it-- is long and floppy and ugly. At one end, it widens out into this more bulbous shape, like a pink, wrinkly pear. That's the rectum. At the other end, it narrows into a soft, pinkish-white tube.
<v Ben Calhoun>好吧，为了更清楚地描述一下，猪直肠——“bung”是业内实际术语——又长又软，很难看。一端变宽，形状更圆，像个粉红色的、皱巴巴的梨。那就是直肠。另一端变细，成了一根柔软的、粉白色的管子。

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:26.070
<v Ben Calhoun>I know, it sounds gross. But also, consider, we are a nation that eats more than a billion pounds of sausage every year, billion with a B. Maybe you like liverwurst or capicola or summer sausage with a natural casing. Then you, like me, have eaten bung-- stuffed, dried bung.
<v Ben Calhoun>我知道，听起来很恶心。但也得想想，我们国家每年消费超过十亿磅香肠，是“十亿”的十亿。也许你喜欢肝泥香肠、卡皮科拉或者带天然肠衣的夏季香肠。那么，你像我一样，吃过直肠——填充的、干制的直肠。

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:49.690
<v Ben Calhoun>A lot of brats and Italian sausages are stuffed in intestine. So if you eat those regularly, you pretty much live up the street from bung. So why does the idea of a fried ring of bung just feel grosser? Well, partly, it's the visual, right? When you see that little ring of calamari, you don't want to picture it in the context of a pig's behind.
<v Ben Calhoun>很多德国香肠和意大利香肠都是用肠衣填充的。所以如果你经常吃那些，你其实就住在直肠隔壁。那么为什么炸直肠圈的念头感觉更恶心？嗯，部分是因为视觉效果，对吧？当你看到那个小小的鱿鱼圈时，你不想把它和猪屁股联系在一起。

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:56.360
<v Ben Calhoun>Then there are all the people who don't eat pork, period. Ron said there's also another reason.
<v Ben Calhoun>此外还有那些根本不吃猪肉的人。罗恩说还有另一个原因。

00:09:56.360 --> 00:10:09.790
<v Ron Meek>Just because of the word "bung," probably. I mean, people don't just want to jump up and say, man, I'm going to eat me some bung tonight. I mean, that's just the way it is.
<v Ron Meek>可能就是因为“直肠”这个词。我是说，人们不想突然站起来说，伙计，我今晚要吃点直肠。我是说，情况就是这样。

00:10:09.790 --> 00:10:43.720
<v Ben Calhoun>But the big question, the question you've been thinking about since we got on this topic, have you or I eaten imitation calamari, bung dressed up as seafood? Well, Ron didn't know. He said his plant exported a lot of their bung to Asia. But he just didn't know much about whatever happened after it left the door. So he could only speculate. Anything, he said, would be a wild guess.
<v Ben Calhoun>但最大的问题，我们谈论这个话题时你一直在思考的问题：你或我吃过仿制鱿鱼吗？把直肠伪装成海鲜？好吧，罗恩不知道。他说他的工厂把大量的直肠出口到亚洲。但他对这些东西离开工厂后发生的事了解不多。所以他只能猜测。他说，任何说法都只是盲目猜测。

00:10:43.720 --> 00:11:04.460
<v Ben Calhoun>So I turned to people who would know. Is pork bung being falsely peddled as calamari? I called the USDA. The USDA's Food Inspection Service issued the following statement to me. "Products we inspect, including those derived from pork, must be accurately labeled and cannot purport to be a product of another species."
<v Ben Calhoun>所以我转向了那些应该知道的人。猪直肠被虚假冒充为鱿鱼了吗？我联系了美国农业部。美国农业部食品检验局给我发表了以下声明：“我们检验的产品，包括猪肉制品，必须标注准确，不能冒充其他物种的产品。”

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:28.050
<v Ben Calhoun>So it's against the rules. But people break the rules. A recent study of seafood by a group called Oceana used DNA testing, and found that all across the country, fish is regularly being labeled as other species in restaurants and in grocery stores, escolar sold as white tuna, Pacific rockfish being fraudulently sold as snapper.
<v Ben Calhoun>所以这是违规的。但人们会违反规定。最近由一家名为Oceana的组织进行的一项海鲜研究，使用了DNA检测，发现全国各地的餐馆和杂货店里，鱼类经常被标为其他物种：无须鳕被当成白金枪鱼卖，太平洋岩鱼被欺诈性地作为红鲷鱼销售。

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:49.260
<v Ben Calhoun>In Miami, more than 30% of fish was being sold as something it wasn't. In New York, the number was 39%, Boston, 48%, Los Angeles-- are you ready? 55%, 55. That means if you order fish in LA, you are most likely eating a species you did not order.
<v Ben Calhoun>在迈阿密，超过30%的鱼类名不副实。在纽约，这个数字是39%，波士顿48%，洛杉矶——准备好了吗？55%，55。这意味着如果你在洛杉矶点鱼，你很有可能吃的是你没点的物种。

00:11:49.260 --> 00:12:11.490
<v Ben Calhoun>In other words, seafood substitution is rampant in this country. And depending on where you live, from what I can tell, you can get cleaned hog bung for about half the price of clean squid. So there would be money in it if you could pull off the switch. And as best as I can tell, were you to do this, you would not be caught.
<v Ben Calhoun>换句话说，海鲜替换在我国很猖獗。根据你的居住地，据我所知，你可以以清洁鱿鱼价格的一半买到干净的猪直肠。所以如果你能成功转换，是有利可图的。而且据我所知，即使你这么做，也不会被抓到。

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:33.700
<v Ben Calhoun>A lawyer who's familiar with this area of law and regulation told me, once bung leaves the plant, there's a variety of agencies and entities that would be in play-- USDA, FDA, state, and local government. But ultimately, he said, the regulation we have is not designed to catch an offense like this. It's aimed mostly at sanitation and food safety.
<v Ben Calhoun>一位熟悉这一领域法律和法规的律师告诉我，一旦直肠离开工厂，就会有各种机构和实体参与——农业部、食品药品监督管理局、州政府和地方政府。但最终，他说，我们现有的监管并非旨在查获此类违法行为。它的重点主要在于卫生和食品安全。

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:53.090
<v Ben Calhoun>So bottom line, the lawyer said, if somebody wanted to do it, chances are, they'd get away with it. So is someone out there doing this? Well, for weeks, I looked for an answer. The USDA says they've never heard of anyone trying to pass pork bung as squid or any other species.
<v Ben Calhoun>所以底线是，律师说，如果有人想这么做，他们很可能会侥幸逃脱。那么有人在这么做吗？几周以来，我一直在寻找答案。美国农业部表示，他们从未听说过有人试图把猪直肠当成鱿鱼或任何其他物种来售卖。

00:12:53.090 --> 00:12:55.120
<v Ben Calhoun>[PHONE RINGING]
<v Ben Calhoun>（电话铃声）

00:12:55.120 --> 00:13:00.590
<v Ben Calhoun>I contacted the National Restaurant Association, the National Pork Producers Council, the National Pork Board--
<v Ben Calhoun>我联系了全美餐馆协会、全国猪肉生产商委员会、国家猪肉委员会——

00:13:00.590 --> 00:13:00.880
<v Woman On Phone>Hello?
<v Woman On Phone>喂？

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:02.360
<v Ben Calhoun>--a squid fisherman's association--
<v Ben Calhoun>——一个鱿鱼渔民协会——

00:13:02.360 --> 00:13:03.190
<v Woman On Phone>Good afternoon.
<v Woman On Phone>下午好。

00:13:03.190 --> 00:13:07.250
<v Ben Calhoun>--Cisco and other big food and restaurant supply companies.
<v Ben Calhoun>——思科以及其他大型食品和餐饮供应公司。

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:09.440
<v Woman On Phone>It's hog what?
<v Woman On Phone>它是猪什么？

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:11.560
<v Ben Calhoun>Bung, hog rectum.
<v Ben Calhoun>直肠，猪肛门。

00:13:11.560 --> 00:13:12.970
<v Woman On Phone>Oh, my gosh.
<v Woman On Phone>噢，天呐。

00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:27.310
<v Ben Calhoun>That's the executive director of the California Wet Fish Producers Association. But the answer was pretty much always the same, nobody had heard of it. But almost to a person, they added that that doesn't mean it's not happening somewhere.
<v Ben Calhoun>那是加州湿鱼生产商协会的执行董事。但答案几乎总是一样的，没人听说过。但几乎每个人都补充说，这并不意味着它不在某处发生。

00:13:27.310 --> 00:13:56.240
<v Ben Calhoun>Ron Meeks said a lot of the bung from his plant got exported. So my next call was to the US Meat Export Federation, which confirmed that, quote, "the main destinations for pork bungs are China, Hong Kong, Japan, Taiwan, Korea, and the Philippines. They are mainly used for processing. But we are aware of some uses in soups and certain entrees. We are not aware of them being used as a substitute for calamari, but it's not impossible," unquote.
<v Ben Calhoun>罗恩·米克说他工厂的很多直肠都出口了。所以我的下一个电话是打给美国肉类出口联合会，他们证实了这一点，引用如下：“猪直肠的主要目的地是中国、香港、日本、台湾、韩国和菲律宾。它们主要用于深加工。但我们知道它们在汤和某些主菜中的一些用法。我们不知道它们被用作鱿鱼的替代品，但并非不可能，”引文结束。

00:13:56.240 --> 00:14:18.890
<v Ben Calhoun>So over the past few weeks, I've called Asian food suppliers, people who live in, work in, and eat in those countries. I talked to a woman named Corinne Trang, who's written an overarching compendium of Asian cuisines. I've talked to academics at NYU and Haverford and USC and Harvard. I've reached out to chefs who know Asian food.
<v Ben Calhoun>所以过去几周，我给亚洲食品供应商、住在那些国家、在那些国家工作和吃饭的人打了电话。我和一位名叫科琳·特朗的女士聊过，她写过一本亚洲菜肴的综合纲要。我跟纽约大学、哈弗福德学院、南加州大学和哈佛大学的学者谈过。我联系了了解亚洲美食的厨师。

00:14:18.890 --> 00:14:46.560
<v Ben Calhoun>The answer, again, always similar-- never heard of it, but it's possible, partly because bung doesn't have such a complicated reputation in Asia, where it has to be some kind of secret ingredient, like it does here. On the other hand, though, people pointed out that in Korea and Japan, you can't get more than a few hours from the ocean. Squid is cheap and it's readily available. You'd only eat a substitute if you wanted the substitute.
<v Ben Calhoun>答案再次相似——没听说过，但有可能，部分原因是直肠在亚洲没有那么复杂的名声，它不需要像在这里那样成为某种秘密配料。然而另一方面，人们指出，在韩国和日本，离海洋最远也就几个小时。鱿鱼既便宜又容易买到。只有当你想要这种替代品时，你才会去吃它。

00:14:46.560 --> 00:15:09.090
<v Ben Calhoun>Generally, people said, if the switch was happening somewhere, they'd guess China. Eventually, I found my way to this guy, who I was really excited about, someone who I thought might have my answer. He was, get this, an anthropologist who lived and worked in China for 40 years, where he studied food, and specifically, meat.
<v Ben Calhoun>人们普遍表示，如果这种交换在某处发生，他们会猜测是中国。最终，我找到了这个人，我对此非常兴奋，我认为他可能有我的答案。他是一位在中国的大学里生活和工作了40年的人类学家，他专门研究食物，尤其是肉类。

00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:36.060
<v Ben Calhoun>When I talked to him, though, he made two points. Point one, my question about this happening in Asia was racist. Even just asking the question was racist because it plays on ignorant stereotypes about other cultures eating things that we perceive as weird. Point two was that Ron Meek, my guy from the pig plant, Ron was pulling my leg. And he was getting away with it because I was a dumbass.
<v Ben Calhoun>但当我跟他交谈时，他指出了两点。第一点，我关于这在亚洲发生的问题是种族主义。仅仅是问这个问题就是种族主义，因为它利用了关于其他文化吃我们认为奇怪东西的无知刻板印象。第二点是，罗恩·米克，我那位从猪肉加工厂找来的人，是在逗我玩。而他能得逞是因为我是个白痴。

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:50.620
<v Ben Calhoun>He told me more than once that I should, quote, "find something worthwhile to do with myself." When we ended our conversation, he told me that he was refusing to even dignify what I was doing by appearing on the radio or by letting me use his name.
<v Ben Calhoun>他不止一次告诉我，我应该“找点有价值的事情做”。当我们结束谈话时，他告诉我，他甚至拒绝通过上广播或让我使用他的名字来证明我所做的事情的正当性。

00:15:50.620 --> 00:16:13.750
<v Ben Calhoun>OK, so to respond to his points one by one, first, am I racist against Asians? Well, I'm half Chinese. My mom's Chinese. Like anyone, I've had the occasional issue with my mother. But this has not been one of them. We grew up eating chicken feet and fish eyes. And I think it's possible to raise the question of who eats what without being racist.
<v Ben Calhoun>好吧，逐一回应他的观点。首先，我对亚洲人有种族歧视吗？嗯，我有一半中国血统。我妈妈是中国人。像任何人一样，我和母亲偶尔会有问题。但这并不是其中之一。我们从小吃鸡爪和鱼眼。我觉得提出谁吃什么的问题而不带有种族主义是可能的。

00:16:13.750 --> 00:16:23.500
<v Ben Calhoun>His second point, though, that Ron Meek was pulling my leg, I mean, the guy was still an expert on meat in China. So I called Ron back.
<v Ben Calhoun>不过他的第二点，即罗恩·米克在逗我玩，我是说，那个人毕竟是中国肉类方面的专家。所以我又给罗恩打了电话。

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:25.790
<v Ron Meek>All right, shoot me some questions, dude.
<v Ron Meek>好的，尽管问我问题，老兄。

00:16:25.790 --> 00:16:30.670
<v Ben Calhoun>I told him the whole thing, about the anthropologist, about what he said.
<v Ben Calhoun>我把整件事告诉了他，关于那位人类学家，关于他说的那些话。

00:16:30.670 --> 00:16:35.270
<v Ben Calhoun>I guess, I mean, the only thing I want to ask you is, are you messing with me?
<v Ben Calhoun>我想，我是说，我唯一想问你的是，你是在耍我吗？

00:16:35.270 --> 00:16:48.530
<v Ron Meek>No. I mean, that was what my boss told me. I was like, what the hell do we save these hog bungs for? He says they use them for imitation squids and stuff like that.
<v Ron Meek>没有。我是说，那是我的老板告诉我的。我当时问，我们为什么要留这些猪直肠？他说他们把这些用作仿制鱿鱼之类的东西。

00:16:48.530 --> 00:16:52.220
<v Ben Calhoun>So in your heart of hearts, you believe it?
<v Ben Calhoun>所以在你的心底深处，你相信它是真的？

00:16:52.220 --> 00:17:17.810
<v Ron Meek>Yeah, man, I mean, I ain't going to sit here and tell you things that, they're just bull-[BLEEP], and play with you, when I'm just going off of my knowledge of saving hog bungs. I mean, you got to think about how far advanced slaughterhouses are, especially big ones, that want to make every penny count.
<v Ron Meek>是啊，伙计，我是说，我不会坐在这里跟你胡扯，逗你玩，我只是根据我保存猪直肠的知识说的。我是说，你得想想屠宰场有多先进，特别是那些大厂，它们想赚取每一分钱。

00:17:17.810 --> 00:17:43.680
<v Ron Meek>The one I worked at, you bring the pigs in, you stun them, then you stick them, and the blood goes off into a trough, and it goes down. And it's vacuum sucked out of there with a vacuum, into centrifuges. And they separate the blood from the blood plasma. And they save that.
<v Ron Meek>我工作的那一家，你把猪赶进去，电晕它们，然后刺穿它们，血流进槽里，流下去。它是用真空吸出来的，进入离心机。它们把血从血浆中分离出来。它们保存那个。

00:17:43.680 --> 00:18:10.890
<v Ron Meek>I mean, they save the lungs. They save the pancreas. They save the spleens. They save the hearts. The only thing left, by the time it's all said and done, is a skull and jawbones. I mean, you can be an anthropologist all you want. And if you don't work in a processing plant, you don't know [BLEEP].
<v Ron Meek>我是说，它们保存肺。它们保存胰腺。它们保存脾脏。它们保存心脏。到最后，只剩下头骨和颌骨。我是说，你尽可以当人类学家。如果你不在加工厂工作，你什么都不知道。

00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:28.200
<v Ben Calhoun>I contacted the plant Ron worked at, where this happened. And for what it's worth, they backed him up. They said their sales team had heard of people eating pork bung as imitation calamari, though they hadn't witnessed it firsthand or heard it directly from a customer. It was all hearsay.
<v Ben Calhoun>我联系了罗恩工作的工厂，那是事情发生的地方。不管有没有用，他们支持他的说法。他们说他们的销售团队确实听说过人们把猪直肠当作仿制鱿鱼吃，尽管他们没有亲眼见过或直接从顾客那里听到过。这都是道听途说。

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:57.560
<v Ben Calhoun>So at the end of all this, I still had no proof that anyone was passing off bung as squid. And then I realized I hadn't asked the more basic question here. Could bung do it? Could it pass as calamari? And that question led me to a guy named Eddie Lin. Eddie Lin has eaten a lot of bung, at least a hundred times, he said, probably more. Eddie has an extreme food blog called "Deep-End Dining" and an online TV show called Kamikaze Kitchen.
<v Ben Calhoun>所以这一切结束时，我依然没有证据表明有人把直肠当鱿鱼卖。然后我意识到我还没问这里更基本的问题。直肠能行吗？它能冒充鱿鱼吗？这个问题引导我找到了一个叫埃迪·林的人。埃迪·林吃过很多直肠，他说至少有一百次，可能更多。埃迪有一个名为“深端餐饮”的极限美食博客和一个名为“神风厨房”的网络电视节目。

00:18:57.560 --> 00:19:01.790
<v Eddie Lin>I can definitely see a resemblance, texture-wise.
<v Eddie Lin>我绝对能看出它们在质地上的相似之处。

00:19:01.790 --> 00:19:02.900
<v Ben Calhoun>Oh, really?
<v Ben Calhoun>噢，真的吗？

00:19:02.900 --> 00:19:10.230
<v Eddie Lin>Yeah, yeah, definitely. There's a rubbery texture, sort of like a calamari.
<v Eddie Lin>是的，是的，绝对有。有一种橡胶质感，有点像鱿鱼。

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:11.690
<v Ben Calhoun>Huh.
<v Ben Calhoun>哈。

00:19:11.690 --> 00:19:23.600
<v Eddie Lin>But you would really have to get rid of that, needless to say, foul flavor and odor from the bung.
<v Eddie Lin>但毫无疑问，你真的必须去掉那种直肠特有的腥臭味。

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:32.840
<v Ben Calhoun>Somehow, I hadn't figured that the bung, once it was scrubbed and rinsed and cleaned with steam, that it would still taste like, you know.
<v Ben Calhoun>不知何故，我没预料到直肠在被冲洗干净并用蒸汽清洁后，还会尝起来像那个……你知道的。

00:19:32.840 --> 00:19:42.930
<v Eddie Lin>So yeah, you would definitely have to do some major, major blanching-- or brining. I meant brining.
<v Eddie Lin>所以是的，你绝对得做一些重大的、重大的焯水——或者盐渍。我是说盐渍。

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:47.010
<v Ben Calhoun>Uh-huh, to just to try and leach those flavors out of there?
<v Ben Calhoun>嗯哼，就是为了试图把那些味道洗掉？

00:19:47.010 --> 00:19:54.710
<v Eddie Lin>Yeah, I mean, those flavors have been marinating in that pig for quite a while. So--
<v Eddie Lin>是啊，我是说，那些味道已经在猪身上腌制了相当长一段时间了。所以——

00:19:54.710 --> 00:19:55.350
<v Ben Calhoun>A lifetime?
<v Ben Calhoun>一辈子？

00:19:55.350 --> 00:19:55.580
<v Eddie Lin>Yeah.
<v Eddie Lin>是的。

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:57.700
<v Eddie Lin>[LAUGHS]
<v Eddie Lin>（笑）

00:19:57.700 --> 00:20:20.630
<v Ben Calhoun>He thought it wouldn't be easy, but he thought it could be done. And there was only one way to tell if he was right, to cook up some bung and eat it. And if the taste was overwhelming and the texture was all wrong, well, then I'd have my answer. And at this point, I'll be frank, I started to root for the bung.
<v Ben Calhoun>他认为这不容易，但他认为可以做到。要判断他说得对不对，只有一个办法，那就是烹饪一些直肠吃掉。如果味道让人难以忍受，质地也完全不对，那我就有答案了。到这时，坦白说，我开始为直肠加油了。

00:20:20.630 --> 00:20:58.410
<v Ben Calhoun>I realized that this is not a story about fraud. It's not a bait-and-switch story. It's a story about possibility. It's classic rags to riches. It's about whether a cut of meat, perhaps the lowliest, most malignable cut of meat in America, might somehow, in at least one place on the planet, be dipped in the redemptive oils of the great culinary equalizer that is the deep fryer. And it might emerge transformed, no longer an outcast, but instead, hair combed, clean-shaven, in a suit and tie. It might walk reborn onto a table.
<v Ben Calhoun>我意识到这不是一个关于欺诈的故事。这不是一个诱骗的故事。这是一个关于可能性的故事。它是经典的“从贫穷到富有”。这是关于一种肉，也许是美国最卑微、最令人难堪的肉，是否能在地球上的某个地方，浸入深炸锅这种伟大的烹饪平衡器的红色洗礼油中。它可能会脱胎换骨，不再是个被放逐者，而是梳洗干净，穿着西装打着领带。它可能会重获新生出现在餐桌上。

00:20:58.410 --> 00:21:34.400
<v Ben Calhoun>Through sheer force of resemblance, it might be loved, its history, years of drudgery and hardship, doing the body's least glamorous job, all washed away. No, this is not the story of a con man like Bernie Madoff. It's Pretty Woman. This is whether Good Will Hunting finds his way out of Southie. It's whether Charlie, on that very last chocolate bar, really can get a golden ticket.
<v Ben Calhoun>凭借相似的力量，它可能会被喜爱，它的历史，那些年的苦役和艰辛，做着身体最不光彩的工作，全都一洗而空。不，这不是伯尼·麦道夫那样的骗子故事。这是《漂亮女人》。这是关于《心灵捕手》中的查理是否能找到走出南波士顿的路。这是关于查理在最后那块巧克力里是否真的能得到金奖券。

00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:55.760
<v Ben Calhoun>To do all this, to try it, I called my little sister, Lauren. She's a chef, trained abroad at The Cordon Bleu, worked at Michelin-star restaurants. She's that kind of chef. Soon, we were standing in front of a deli case. And I don't know why I feel hesitant about saying this because I don't think it's racist. It was in Chinatown.
<v Ben Calhoun>为了做这一切，为了尝试，我打电话给我的小妹妹劳伦。她是一位厨师，在蓝带学院进修过，在米其林星级餐厅工作过。她是那种水平的厨师。很快，我们就站在一个熟食柜前。我不知道为什么我提到这一点时感到犹豫，因为我不认为这是种族主义。那是在唐人街。

00:21:55.760 --> 00:22:02.960
<v Lauren>Pork bung, there it is. Look at it, it looks like a sphincter.
<v Lauren>猪直肠，就在那儿。看看它，它看起来像括约肌。

00:22:02.960 --> 00:22:04.920
<v Ben Calhoun>Wait, what do you mean, it looks like a--
<v Ben Calhoun>等等，你是指它看起来像个——

00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:08.010
<v Lauren>This one up here, that's cut up, it looks like a butt hole.
<v Lauren>上面这个，切开的，看起来像个屁眼。

00:22:08.010 --> 00:22:10.780
<v Lauren>[LAUGHS]
<v Lauren>（笑）

00:22:10.780 --> 00:22:12.020
<v Ben Calhoun>It does.
<v Ben Calhoun>的确。

00:22:12.020 --> 00:22:24.290
<v Ira Glass>And Lauren had theories about pulling it off-- brining, soaking, maybe braising. But once we got to the store, once she'd seen the meat up close, her doubts got worse.
<v Ira Glass>劳伦对如何做到这一点有理论——盐渍、浸泡，也许是炖煮。但一旦我们到了商店，一旦她近距离看到这些肉，她的疑虑就加深了。

00:22:24.290 --> 00:22:30.450
<v Lauren>I think, after looking at it, I don't think it's going to--
<v Lauren>我想，看过之后，我不觉得它会——

00:22:30.450 --> 00:22:31.750
<v Ben Calhoun>You don't think it's going to work?
<v Ben Calhoun>你不觉得它会成功？

00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:47.100
<v Lauren>No. It's too thick. There's too much muscle tissue. It's too thick. You'd have to use a ring cutter to make it the right thickness.
<v Lauren>不。它太厚了。肌肉组织太多。它太厚了。你得用圆环切割机把它切成合适的厚度。

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:50.310
<v Ben Calhoun>What do you think those bits are, in there?
<v Ben Calhoun>你觉得里面的那些碎块是什么？

00:22:50.310 --> 00:22:52.560
<v Lauren>Oh, you know, poo.
<v Lauren>噢，你知道，屎。

00:22:52.560 --> 00:23:07.170
<v Ben Calhoun>My sister said Eddie Lin was definitely right. The giveaway would be the stubborn flavor of poo. That flavor, she said, it's tough to get rid of. The Earth revolves around the sun. And bung will always taste like [BLEEP].
<v Ben Calhoun>我妹妹说埃迪·林绝对是对的。败笔就是那种挥之不去的屎味。她说，那种味道很难去掉。地球绕着太阳转。而直肠永远尝起来像（哔）。

00:23:07.170 --> 00:23:42.160
<v Ben Calhoun>But there was no backing out now. We would eat. We would eat our way to the truth. And so what if it didn't look good? So what if bung was destined to taste like bung? You know who it didn't look good for, and he still put up a fight? Rocky Balboa, that's who. This was it, the bung versus calamari, squid versus tail, The Rumble in the Bunghole.
<v Ben Calhoun>但现在没有退路了。我们会吃。我们要通过吃来找到真相。如果它看起来不好看又怎样？如果直肠注定要尝起来像直肠又怎样？你知道谁看起来情况不好，却依然抗争吗？洛奇·巴尔博亚，就是他。这就是了，直肠对阵鱿鱼，鱿鱼对阵尾巴，直肠里的隆隆声。

00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:57.610
<v Ben Calhoun>We set up the tasting at a restaurant, in the lull between lunch and dinner. In the dining room, there was just a few tables eating. And all around the restaurant, the morning shift was wrapping up as we walked in with a red cooler filled with squid and hog bung.
<v Ben Calhoun>我们在午餐和晚餐之间的空档期设置了品尝环节。餐厅里只有几桌客人在用餐。当我们拎着装满鱿鱼和猪直肠的红色冷藏箱走进来时，整个餐厅的早班工作人员正在收尾。

00:23:57.610 --> 00:24:08.740
<v Ben Calhoun>So originally, I'd recruited some half a dozen people from our office at This American Life as tasters. The final group the day of the tasting included, from the office, Seth Lind and Brian Reed.
<v Ben Calhoun>最初，我从我们的《美国生活》办公室里招募了大约半打人作为评委。品尝当天的最终小组成员包括办公室的塞斯·林德和布莱恩·里德。

00:24:08.740 --> 00:24:13.180
<v Ben Calhoun>So when's the last time that you guys had calamari?
<v Ben Calhoun>你们上次吃鱿鱼是什么时候？

00:24:13.180 --> 00:24:20.470
<v Seth Lind>I had calamari probably a month ago.
<v Seth Lind>我大概一个月前吃了鱿鱼。

00:24:20.470 --> 00:24:23.180
<v Ben Calhoun>And what about you, Brian?
<v Ben Calhoun>那你呢，布莱恩？

00:24:23.180 --> 00:24:29.540
<v Brian Reed>I haven't had it since we got this tip about the possible--
<v Brian Reed>自从我们得到这个可能的线索后，我就没吃过——

00:24:29.540 --> 00:24:31.140
<v Ben Calhoun>Oh, my gosh, have you been avoiding?
<v Ben Calhoun>噢，天呐，你一直在避开它吗？

00:24:31.140 --> 00:24:42.890
<v Brian Reed>Yeah, no, totally. And to be honest, I ate calamari pretty regular. I'm a regular eater of calamari-- not all the time, but it's something I'll routinely order if it's on an appetizer menu.
<v Brian Reed>是的，完全避开了。老实说，我以前经常吃鱿鱼。我是鱿鱼的常客——不是一直吃，但如果开胃菜菜单上有它，我会习惯性地点。

00:24:42.890 --> 00:24:43.640
<v Ben Calhoun>Yeah.
<v Ben Calhoun>对。

00:24:43.640 --> 00:24:55.670
<v Brian Reed>I mean, I grew up in an Italian-American family, where my grandparents were also born here. I feel like calamari is just big among that sector of people, at Olive Garden and stuff like that. So I just grew up eating it.
<v Brian Reed>我是说，我在一个意大利裔美国人家庭长大，我的祖父母也是在这里出生的。我觉得鱿鱼在这些人中间很受欢迎，比如在橄榄花园餐厅之类的。所以我从小就吃它。

00:24:55.670 --> 00:25:01.950
<v Ben Calhoun>I hadn't realized this. For weeks, Brian had been avoiding calamari. He'd been living in fear.
<v Ben Calhoun>我没意识到这一点。几个星期以来，布莱恩一直在避开鱿鱼。他一直生活在恐惧中。

00:25:01.950 --> 00:25:06.980
<v Ben Calhoun>Brian, if you find out that they're indiscernible from each other, will you ever eat calamari again?
<v Ben Calhoun>布莱恩，如果你发现它们无法区分，你还会再吃鱿鱼吗？

00:25:06.980 --> 00:25:12.030
<v Brian Reed>No, I don't think so. That's why I want to do this, just to know, going forward.
<v Brian Reed>不，我不觉得。这就是我想做这个的原因，就是为了知道未来的情况。

00:25:12.030 --> 00:25:35.200
<v Ben Calhoun>Back in the kitchen, things were looking bad. I'd given up on the idea that bung would taste the same as calamari. Now I'd hung my hopes on the idea that at least visually, it would look the same. But as my sister dropped the flowered rings of hog bung into the fryer, they had turned into this kind of big, ugly, tangled wad, nothing like the jiggly squid rings.
<v Ben Calhoun>回到厨房，情况看起来很不妙。我已经放弃了直肠尝起来和鱿鱼一样的念头。现在我寄希望于它至少在视觉上看起来是一样的。但当我的妹妹把裹了面糊的猪直肠圈放进油锅里时，它们变成了一种巨大的、丑陋的、纠结的一团，一点也不像那些有弹性的鱿鱼圈。

00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:40.960
<v Ben Calhoun>Oh, they're very scraggly-looking.
<v Ben Calhoun>噢，它们看起来非常破烂。

00:25:40.960 --> 00:25:52.870
<v Ira Glass>But then, as if by a miracle, they changed. My sister gave a shake to the fry basket, and as they sizzled, the bung just seemed to gracefully snap into rings.
<v Ira Glass>但接着，仿佛奇迹发生，它们变了。我妹妹摇了摇炸篮，随着它们滋滋作响，直肠似乎优雅地弹成了圆圈。

00:25:52.870 --> 00:25:57.890
<v Ben Calhoun>Oh, look at that, though. It's like magic. They're turning into circles.
<v Ben Calhoun>噢，看那个，太棒了。就像魔法。它们正在变成圆圈。

00:25:57.890 --> 00:26:02.440
<v Lauren>Yeah. So I'm going to pull these first ones.
<v Lauren>是啊。所以我先把这第一批捞出来。

00:26:02.440 --> 00:26:16.070
<v Ben Calhoun>Soon, we were face to face with the plate. On it, there were two piles of rings, similar in size, similar in shape. The bung had more of a frizzly edge to it, kind of like a fancy onion ring. The calamari was smoother.
<v Ben Calhoun>很快，我们面对着盘子。上面有两堆圈，大小相似，形状相似。直肠的边缘更多毛，有点像高档的洋葱圈。鱿鱼的更光滑。

00:26:16.070 --> 00:26:16.750
<v Ben Calhoun>OK, so--
<v Ben Calhoun>好的，那么——

00:26:16.750 --> 00:26:19.580
<v Ira Glass>I asked Seth and Brian to just give a first impression.
<v Ira Glass>我让塞斯和布莱恩先谈谈第一印象。

00:26:19.580 --> 00:26:25.600
<v Brian Reed>I have a guess. But it's one of those things where you're pretty sure, but you could totally be wrong.
<v Brian Reed>我有猜想。但这是那种你很确定，但却完全可能弄错的情况。

00:26:25.600 --> 00:26:43.620
<v Seth Lind>God, I thought I would be more sure. I don't-- I'm waffling now. My gut reaction is that this was calamari and this was not.
<v Seth Lind>天呐，我以为我会更确定。我不知道……我现在很犹豫。我的直觉反应是，这个是鱿鱼，这个不是。

00:26:43.620 --> 00:26:47.900
<v Brian Reed>OK, before you eat it, see, I totally thought this was calamari, the other one.
<v Brian Reed>好的，在你吃之前，看，我完全以为这一堆是鱿鱼。

00:26:47.900 --> 00:26:48.350
<v Seth Lind>OK.
<v Seth Lind>好的。

00:26:48.350 --> 00:26:50.620
<v Brian Reed>So I'm going to do it at the same time.
<v Brian Reed>所以我打算同时吃。

00:26:50.620 --> 00:27:02.770
<v Ben Calhoun>OK, so just to be clear what's going on here, Seth has chosen one pile of rings which he thinks is calamari. Brian has done the same thing, only Brian is choosing the other stack of fried rings.
<v Ben Calhoun>好的，为了澄清一下这里发生了什么，塞斯挑选了一堆他认为是鱿鱼的圈。布莱恩也做了同样的事，只是他选的是另一堆炸圈。

00:27:02.770 --> 00:27:09.440
<v Seth Lind>OK, so we're about to bite into these simultaneously, which we both think this is calamari.
<v Seth Lind>好的，我们要同时咬下去，我们都认为这是鱿鱼。

00:27:09.440 --> 00:27:10.190
<v Brian Reed>But they're the opposite ones.
<v Brian Reed>但它们是相反的。

00:27:10.190 --> 00:27:12.300
<v Seth Lind>The opposite ones. OK.
<v Seth Lind>相反的。好的。

00:27:12.300 --> 00:27:18.880
<v Seth Lind>[CRUNCHING]
<v Seth Lind>（咀嚼声）

00:27:18.880 --> 00:27:59.660
<v Ben Calhoun>So in actuality, Seth is right. Seth is eating calamari. The chewing you hear from Brian's mic, that is the sound of a calamari lover eating fried pig rectum. I should also add there were actually two varieties of bung on the plate that day, one bung that my sister had blanched over and over to mellow any organ-y, fecal flavor, and then untreated, straight-up bung, unfiltered, unchained, uncut, 100% pure bung. That one, the latter one, bung at its purest, at the height of its bunginess, this is what Brian was eating. As they ate, Seth still looked confident.
<v Ben Calhoun>所以事实上，塞斯对了。塞斯吃的是鱿鱼。你从布莱恩麦克风里听到的咀嚼声，是一个鱿鱼爱好者在吃炸猪直肠的声音。我还要补充一点，那天盘子里实际上有两种直肠，一种是我妹妹反复焯水以减轻任何器官腥味、粪便味处理过的，另一种是未处理的、直截了当的直肠，未过滤、未受限、未切割，100%纯直肠。后者，那种最纯粹的、直肠味最浓的，就是布莱恩在吃的。当他们吃的时候，塞斯看起来依然很自信。

00:27:59.660 --> 00:28:02.970
<v Seth Lind>I think I was right.
<v Seth Lind>我想我对了。

00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:04.400
<v Brian Reed>(LAUGHING) I think I was right.
<v Brian Reed>（笑）我想我对了。

00:28:04.400 --> 00:28:05.090
<v Ben Calhoun>Really?
<v Ben Calhoun>真的吗？

00:28:05.090 --> 00:28:09.080
<v Brian Reed>Yeah. I totally think I was right.
<v Brian Reed>是的。我完全认为我对了。

00:28:09.080 --> 00:28:32.610
<v Ben Calhoun>Game, set, match, bung. And it wasn't just Brian. I thought so. Damien, who manages the restaurant, he also thought it was passable. A few of us picked up a faint flavor of pork rind, but if you weren't really looking for it, you wouldn't notice it was there. One of the restaurant staff, a guy named Ethan Vanburen, had the simplest, clearest explanation.
<v Ben Calhoun>局、盘、赛，直肠赢。不仅仅是布莱恩。我也这么认为。餐厅经理达米恩也认为这可以蒙混过关。我们中有几个人闻到了淡淡的猪油味，但如果你不是真的在找，你不会注意到它在那里。餐厅员工之一伊桑·范布伦，给出了最简单、最清晰的解释。

00:28:32.610 --> 00:28:37.390
<v Ethan Vanburen>I think that when you slice something up really thin and deep fry it, it's going to taste like something that's been deep fried.
<v Ethan Vanburen>我觉得当你把东西切得很薄然后深度油炸时，它就会尝起来像某种油炸的东西。

00:28:37.390 --> 00:28:45.250
<v Ben Calhoun>If a plate full of the bung came out, how many people do you think would even-- do you think you'd notice if you were in that setting?
<v Ben Calhoun>如果端上来一盘直肠，你觉得有多少人会注意到？你觉得你在那种环境下会注意到吗？

00:28:45.250 --> 00:28:50.250
<v Ethan Vanburen>I'd say top scenario is somebody says, this calamari tastes funny, and keeps eating it.
<v Ethan Vanburen>我会说最糟糕的情况就是有人说，“这鱿鱼吃起来怪怪的”，然后继续吃。

00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:58.710
<v Ben Calhoun>And as for Brian-- oh, Brian-- Brian was reeling a bit, trying to figure out just what this was going to mean for him.
<v Ben Calhoun>至于布莱恩——噢，布莱恩——他有点晕头转向，试图弄清楚这对意味着什么。

00:28:58.710 --> 00:29:02.370
<v Brian Reed>I'm sure I've been fooled in the past. I'm just, like, sure of it.
<v Brian Reed>我肯定以前被骗过。我只是……就是确定。

00:29:02.370 --> 00:29:05.490
<v Ben Calhoun>Wait, oh, you're thinking that you've been places in the past, and you've had bung?
<v Ben Calhoun>等等，你是说你觉得你以前去过某些地方，吃过直肠？

00:29:05.490 --> 00:29:17.470
<v Brian Reed>I just imagined seeing a plate with this food, that looks like this on it, sitting with my family, growing up. We definitely have eaten something that tasted like this, and just thought it was calamari, for sure.
<v Brian Reed>我只是想象着看到一盘这样的食物，长成这样，坐在家里和家人一起吃。我们绝对吃过这种味道的东西，而且肯定以为是鱿鱼。

00:29:17.470 --> 00:29:32.100
<v Ben Calhoun>Not only wasn't he sure if he'd ever eat calamari again, he didn't want to eat the calamari on the plate in front of him, calamari I guaranteed him, was real.
<v Ben Calhoun>他不仅不确定是否还会再吃鱿鱼，甚至不想吃盘子里面前的鱿鱼，我保证，那是真的鱿鱼。

00:29:32.100 --> 00:30:01.420
<v Ben Calhoun>Just to repeat one last time, I have no proof that anyone, anywhere, has ever tried to pass off pork bung as calamari in a restaurant. All I know is, if you wanted to do it, it would be easy. And I'm choosing to believe that it's happening somewhere, because at some point in working on this story, I stopped identifying with Brian and anyone who might feel ripped off or grossed out by getting imitation instead of the real thing.
<v Ben Calhoun>最后重复一遍，我没有任何证据表明有人、在任何地方，曾试图在餐馆里把猪直肠当作鱿鱼来卖。我只知道，如果你想这样做，这很容易。我选择相信它在某处正在发生，因为在制作这个故事的过程中，我不禁认同了布莱恩和任何因为吃到了替代品而非真品而感到被坑或恶心的人。

00:30:01.420 --> 00:30:32.090
<v Ben Calhoun>Now, I identify with the bung. I'd like to think that somewhere out there, right now, under a heat lamp, a platter is sitting. It's warm, and it's full of promise and transformation and redemption. That's the world that I'll choose to live in for me, for you, for the bung. For the bung in all of us.
<v Ben Calhoun>现在，我认同了直肠。我愿意相信，在某处的加热灯下，正摆着一个盘子。它是温暖的，充满了希望、改变和救赎。那是我选择去生活的世界，为了我，为了你，为了直肠。为了我们每个人身体里的直肠。

00:30:32.090 --> 00:30:34.860
<v Ira Glass>Ben Calhoun, he's one of the producers of our program.
<v Ira Glass>本·卡尔霍恩，他是我们节目的制作人之一。

00:30:34.860 --> 00:30:44.030
<v Fred Armisen>(IMITATING IRA GLASS) Ben Calhoun, he's one of the producers of our program. Thanks to the restaurant Aurora, in Brooklyn, for letting us use their kitchen for our taste test.
<v Fred Armisen>（模仿艾拉·格拉斯）本·卡尔霍恩，他是我们节目的制作人之一。感谢布鲁克林的Aurora餐厅让我们使用他们的厨房进行品尝测试。

00:30:44.030 --> 00:30:45.140
<v Ira Glass>OK.
<v Ira Glass>好的。

00:30:45.140 --> 00:30:48.380
<v Fred Armisen>(IMITATING IRA GLASS) OK, who is this? Wait, who am I?
<v Fred Armisen>（模仿艾拉·格拉斯）好的，这是谁？等等，我是谁？

00:30:48.380 --> 00:30:53.540
<v Ira Glass>Well, coming up, Philadelphia and Afghanistan, doppelgangers?
<v Ira Glass>嗯，即将到来，费城和阿富汗，分身？

00:30:53.540 --> 00:31:03.470
<v Fred Armisen>(IMITATING IRA GLASS) That's in a minute. From Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Fred Armisen>（模仿艾拉·格拉斯）那是一分钟之后。来自芝加哥公共广播电台和公共广播国际公司，我们的节目继续。

00:31:03.470 --> 00:31:07.070
<v Fred Armisen>(IMITATING IRA GLASS) So we're going to start act 2?
<v Fred Armisen>（模仿艾拉·格拉斯）所以我们要开始第二幕了？

00:31:07.070 --> 00:31:10.970
<v Ira Glass>Start here. So we're coming back from the ID break.
<v Ira Glass>从这开始。我们刚从身份识别停顿中回来。

00:31:10.970 --> 00:31:26.460
<v Fred Armisen>(IMITATING IRA GLASS) It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our show, of course, we choose a theme. We bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show, doppelgangers, stories of things that have a double, an evil twin, or a not-so-evil twin.
<v Fred Armisen>（模仿艾拉·格拉斯）这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，我们当然会选择一个主题。我们带给你关于这个主题的不同故事。今天的节目，分身，关于那些有双重身份、邪恶双胞胎或并非那么邪恶的双胞胎的故事。

00:31:26.460 --> 00:31:29.920
<v Ira Glass>Oh, wait, I should just explain. Oh, right, so--
<v Ira Glass>噢，等等，我应该解释一下。噢，对了，所以——

00:31:29.920 --> 00:31:31.000
<v Fred Armisen>(IMITATING IRA GLASS) What?
<v Fred Armisen>（模仿艾拉·格拉斯）什么？

00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:33.020
<v Ira Glass>That was just a note to myself. I should explain.
<v Ira Glass>那只是给自己的笔记。我应该解释。

00:31:33.020 --> 00:31:34.100
<v Fred Armisen>(IMITATING IRA GLASS) No, that was a note to me.
<v Fred Armisen>（模仿艾拉·格拉斯）不，那是给我的笔记。

00:31:34.100 --> 00:31:41.980
<v Fred Armisen>[LAUGHTER]
<v Fred Armisen>（笑声）

00:31:41.980 --> 00:31:49.910
<v Ira Glass>So this is why Fred is here. Fred Armisen is co-hosting the show as me because it's a show about doubles and lookalikes.
<v Ira Glass>所以这就是为什么弗雷德在这里。弗雷德·阿米森作为我来共同主持节目，因为这是一个关于双胞胎和长相相似者的节目。

00:31:49.910 --> 00:31:56.920
<v Fred Armisen>(IMITATING IRA GLASS) Right, but we're both creating the same person, in a sense. And that's what the fun is of--
<v Fred Armisen>（模仿艾拉·格拉斯）对，但我们都在创造同一个人，某种意义上。这就是乐趣所在——

00:31:56.920 --> 00:31:59.100
<v Ira Glass>Oh, that was really good, the way you just did that.
<v Ira Glass>噢，这真的很好，你刚才那样做的方式。

00:31:59.100 --> 00:32:00.550
