<v Ira Glass>（笑）

00:32:00.550 --> 00:32:01.890
<v Ira Glass>Finish your thought.
<v Ira Glass>说完你的想法。

00:32:01.890 --> 00:32:03.580
<v Fred Armisen>I didn't have an end to that thought. I just--
<v Fred Armisen>我没有结束那个想法。我只是——

00:32:03.580 --> 00:32:04.360
<v Ira Glass>You just knew that--
<v Ira Glass>你只是知道——

00:32:04.360 --> 00:32:08.410
<v Fred Armisen>I like when you do the little self-- the little discovery.
<v Fred Armisen>我喜欢你做那个小自我发现的时候。

00:32:08.410 --> 00:32:23.520
<v Ira Glass>All right, well, we have arrived at Act 2 of our program, Act 2, In Country and City. So for decades now, the writer Alex Kotlowitz has been writing about the inner cities, and especially the toll of violence on young people. He's probably best known for his book, There Are No Children Here. He appears on our show from time to time.
<v Ira Glass>好的，我们已经到达了节目的第二幕，第二幕，在乡村和城市。几十年来，作家亚历克斯·科特洛维茨一直在描写内城，特别是暴力对年轻人的代价。他可能最出名的是他的书《这里没有孩子》。他偶尔出现在我们的节目中。

00:32:23.520 --> 00:32:34.960
<v Fred Armisen>(IMITATING IRA GLASS) Recently, he heard about an unusual program at Drexel University in Philadelphia, where they're giving guys from inner city neighborhoods counseling for symptoms of PTSD.
<v Fred Armisen>（模仿艾拉·格拉斯）最近，他听说费城德雷塞尔大学有一个不寻常的项目，他们为来自内城社区的人们提供PTSD（创伤后应激障碍）症状的咨询。

00:32:34.960 --> 00:32:48.470
<v Ira Glass>And this interested Alex, because for a long time, he has wondered whether the violence that he was reporting on in Chicago and its effect on kids and adults was comparable to the effects of trauma that a person experiences at war.
<v Ira Glass>这引起了亚历克斯的兴趣，因为很长一段时间以来，他一直在想，他所报道的芝加哥暴力事件及其对儿童和成人的影响，是否与一个人在战争中经历的创伤影响具有可比性。

00:32:48.470 --> 00:32:59.900
<v Fred Armisen>(IMITATING IRA GLASS) So he knew, and we know, how strange that sounds, to compare being in Afghanistan or Iraq to working a street corner in Chicago or some other city.
<v Fred Armisen>（模仿艾拉·格拉斯）所以他知道，我们也知道，把在阿富汗或伊拉克与在芝加哥或其他城市街头工作相提并论，听起来是多么奇怪。

00:32:59.900 --> 00:33:15.960
<v Ira Glass>So Alex tried to see if it was really comparable by doing a pair of interviews. He talked to this vet from Afghanistan and also a guy from Philadelphia, who's lived in some pretty bad neighborhoods his whole life. Alex wanted to see if they were doubles of some kind for each other.
<v Ira Glass>所以亚历克斯试图通过进行一对访谈来看看这是否真的具有可比性。他采访了这位从阿富汗回来的老兵，还有一位来自费城、一生都住在相当糟糕社区的家伙。亚历克斯想看看他们是否是彼此的某种分身。

00:33:15.960 --> 00:33:24.810
<v Fred Armisen>(IMITATING IRA GLASS) One of these guys is 28, one is 30, and both of them are trying to make sense of what they experienced. Here's Alex.
<v Fred Armisen>（模仿艾拉·格拉斯）这些人中一个是28岁，一个是30岁，他们都在试图理解自己经历过的事情。这是亚历克斯。

00:33:24.810 --> 00:33:40.310
<v Alex Kotlowitz>Here's the first of these two guys, Brandon Caro. Brandon spent a year in Afghanistan. His roughest time was the six months he spent in the eastern part of the country, near the Pakistani border. He was a Navy corpsman, a medic who worked with the Marines.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>这是两人中的第一位，布兰登·卡罗。布兰登在阿富汗待了一年。他最艰难的时期是在该国东部，靠近巴基斯坦边境的地方待的那六个月。他是海军医务兵，是与海军陆战队一起工作的军医。

00:33:40.310 --> 00:34:07.930
<v Brandon Caro>As a teenager, yeah, I certainly lacked discipline. I had been thrown out of my house more times than I can remember. And I was 21 when I enlisted. And really, I enlisted because I had, up to that point, not finished college. And it didn't seem as though I was going to finish college any time soon.
<v Brandon Caro>作为一个少年，是的，我肯定缺乏纪律。我被赶出家的次数多到数不清。我21岁时应征入伍。真的，我参军是因为到那时为止，我没完成大学学业。看起来我也不会在短期内完成大学学业。

00:34:07.930 --> 00:34:19.110
<v Alex Kotlowitz>Then there's Curtis Jefferson. He grew up in a rough neighborhood in North Philly, where he oversaw a small group of guys who sold drugs. He made his first drug sale when he was still in high school.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>然后是柯蒂斯·杰斐逊。他在北费城一个糟糕的社区长大，在那里他监督了一小群贩毒的人。他还在上高中时就进行了第一次毒品交易。

00:34:19.110 --> 00:34:50.900
<v Curtis Jefferson>I was 16. My aunt gave me some money for school. And that point, I purchased some weed and some crack with that, never bought my clothes. After that, I sold all the drugs. And I guess from there, that's where it started. I seen that money come in, and I wasn't asking nobody for no money. Especially with my aunt, I wasn't begging for from her pockets and all that. So from there, it was all she wrote.
<v Curtis Jefferson>我16岁。姑姑给了我一些零花钱。在那一点上，我买了些大麻和快克毒品，从来没买过衣服。从那以后，我卖了所有的毒品。我猜从那时起，一切开始了。我看到钱进来，我不向任何人要钱。特别是对我姑姑，我没求过她的钱。所以从那时起，一切就注定了。

00:34:50.900 --> 00:35:06.230
<v Alex Kotlowitz>OK, they're two completely different people who made very different choices in their lives. But I spoke to each of them, looking for similarities in their experiences. And honestly, there were more than I expected. Here's the first similarity.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>好吧，他们是两个完全不同的人，在生活中做了非常不同的选择。但我与他们每个人都进行了交谈，寻找他们经历中的相似之处。老实说，相似之处比我预期的要多。这是第一个相似之处。

00:35:06.230 --> 00:35:22.610
<v Alex Kotlowitz>For Curtis on the street, and for Brandon in Afghanistan, they could never let their guard down. During his tour, Brandon was charged with training Afghan soldiers. And in May 2007, he heard about Afghan soldiers who attacked their American trainers.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>对于街头的柯蒂斯和在阿富汗的布兰登来说，他们永远不能放松警惕。在服役期间，布兰登负责训练阿富汗士兵。2007年5月，他听说阿富汗士兵袭击了他们的美国教官。

00:35:22.610 --> 00:35:48.090
<v Brandon Caro>They had weapons around us all the time. On convoys, we would have to line him up in the morning and collect their cell phones because we couldn't trust them not to inform on us to Taliban fighters. It was exhausting, trying to keep an eye on the Afghan soldiers and look out for IEDs or snipers.
<v Brandon Caro>他们身边一直有武器。在车队里，我们每天早上都得让他们排队收集他们的手机，因为我们不敢相信他们不会向塔利班武装人员通风报信。这让人筋疲力尽，试图盯着阿富汗士兵，还要提防路边炸弹或狙击手。

00:35:48.090 --> 00:36:04.620
<v Curtis Jefferson>Because you're trying to focus on one thing, you're trying to get money, but the same thing, eventually, somebody's going to come up and test you. Somebody's going to test you. Somebody's going to rob you. Somebody's going to send something to your boys, they're going to get robbed. Somebody's going to send shots your way or something.
<v Curtis Jefferson>因为你试图专注于一件事，你试图赚钱，但同样的事情，最终，有人会来测试你。有人会测试你。有人会抢劫你。有人会给你的弟兄发点什么，他们会被抢劫。有人会向你开枪或者什么的。

00:36:04.620 --> 00:36:17.040
<v Brandon Caro>It felt like a piano could fall on you at any time. That's what it felt like to be on patrol, and especially to be on patrol with the Afghans.
<v Brandon Caro>感觉像钢琴随时会掉在你身上。在巡逻时，特别是在和阿富汗人一起巡逻时，感觉就是这样。

00:36:17.040 --> 00:36:22.480
<v Curtis Jefferson>Because when you're out there, you notice different things-- for one, people with their hands in their pockets.
<v Curtis Jefferson>因为当你出去时，你会注意到不同的事情——首先，人们把手放在口袋里。

00:36:22.480 --> 00:36:26.380
<v Brandon Caro>You're looking for someone that doesn't look right, that doesn't feel right.
<v Brandon Caro>你在寻找看起来不对劲、感觉不对劲的人。

00:36:26.380 --> 00:36:39.250
<v Curtis Jefferson>Another thing, people who've got hoodies on, especially black. There's a certain look, put the hoodie on their face, you can't even see their eyeballs. Come on, it's daytime. I can't see your face. Let me see your eyes or something.
<v Curtis Jefferson>另外，戴着连帽衫的人，特别是黑色的。有一种样子，连帽衫戴在脸上，你甚至看不见他们的眼球。得了吧，这是白天。我看不到你的脸。让我看看你的眼睛或者什么的。

00:36:39.250 --> 00:36:45.670
<v Brandon Caro>And I would watch the way they looked at me, If they would stare back at me, if they would smile at me.
<v Brandon Caro>我会观察他们看我的方式，如果他们盯着我，如果他们对我微笑。

00:36:45.670 --> 00:37:01.410
<v Curtis Jefferson>If a person always got to keep touching their side, you're not going in your pocket, you're touching something. And I've been out there so long, I could tell if somebody's got a gun on them or not.
<v Curtis Jefferson>如果一个人总是摸自己的腰，你不是在掏口袋，你是在摸什么东西。我在外面待得太久了，我能看出某人身上有没有枪。

00:37:01.410 --> 00:37:12.560
<v Brandon Caro>For some reason, it was always a white Toyota Camry that was packed with explosives and driven into a convoy, or driven into a checkpoint.
<v Brandon Caro>由于某种原因，总是一辆塞满炸药的白色丰田凯美瑞被开进车队，或开进检查站。

00:37:12.560 --> 00:37:33.330
<v Curtis Jefferson>Grand Marquis, or what else? Crown Victoria, that's a dope-boy car. When I seen them cars when I was on the streets, it was either get ready, go for the straps, go to the guns, or get out of the way. That might be the hop-out boys, come stick you up.
<v Curtis Jefferson>水星大侯爵，或者还有什么？皇冠维多利亚，那是毒贩的车。当我还在街上看到这些车时，要么做好准备，要么去拿带子，去拿枪，或者躲开。那可能是“跳车帮”，来抢劫你的。

00:37:33.330 --> 00:37:47.770
<v Alex Kotlowitz>Here's something else they shared. They both saw people killed, and then had to figure out how to keep going. The first fatality Brandon ever saw came when a convoy, which he was originally scheduled to be a part of, was hit.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>这是他们共有的另一件事。他们都目睹过人被杀，然后必须想办法继续生活。布兰登目睹的第一次死亡事件发生在原定他也要参加的一个车队遭到袭击时。

00:37:47.770 --> 00:38:28.880
<v Brandon Caro>They brought the casualties into our base. And when we swung open the two doors that opened up into the cab of the truck, I was looking at just a heap, a mass of flesh, and that gray, digital-pattern Army uniform. But there was no form to what I was looking at. I knew that what I was looking at was human. But I didn't know what position the body was in because it had been so badly damaged.
<v Brandon Caro>他们把伤员送到了我们的基地。当我们推开卡车驾驶室的两扇门时，我看到的只是一堆血肉，还有那灰色的、数码迷彩的陆军制服。但我看的东西没有形状。我知道我看到的是人。但我不知道身体处于什么位置，因为它受损太严重了。

00:38:28.880 --> 00:38:52.700
<v Brandon Caro>And so we got into the cab and we started to put him into a bag. And I tried not to look at his face. I remember thinking, don't look at his face, don't look at his face. But I had to, inevitably. It was by far, the most intimate, goriest thing I've ever experienced in my life.
<v Brandon Caro>于是我们进了驾驶室，开始把他放进袋子里。我尽量不看他的脸。我记得心里想，别看他的脸，别看他的脸。但我不得不看，不可避免。这绝对是我一生中经历过的最私密、最血腥的事情。

00:38:52.700 --> 00:39:12.940
<v Curtis Jefferson>My first time seeing somebody shot was my own mother. I was five years old, going on six. We were living in the projects, Blumberg projects. My mom, she just got her degree in nursing. She was a nurse, had a nice little job. She was working to get us out of the projects.
<v Curtis Jefferson>我第一次看到有人被枪杀是我自己的母亲。我当时5岁，快6岁了。我们住在布伦伯格公屋项目。我妈刚拿到护理学位。她是个护士，有一份不错的工作。她正在努力让我们搬出公屋。

00:39:12.940 --> 00:39:33.900
<v Curtis Jefferson>Then one morning, she was going out to work, gave my mom a kiss on the lips, and she told my grandma, I'll see y'all later. I'm going to work. As soon as she went out to work, I just heard all this shooting. By that time, I knew what shooting was. But there it was. She just got caught up in a shootout.
<v Curtis Jefferson>然后一天早上，她要去上班，亲了亲我的唇，告诉祖母，我会晚点见你们。我要去上班了。她刚一出门，我就听到所有的枪声。那时候，我知道什么是枪声。但它就在那里。她只是被卷入了一场枪战。

00:39:33.900 --> 00:39:53.640
<v Curtis Jefferson>Somebody knocked on the door. She told my grandma, she said, Barbara's laying on the ground. All I know is my grandma was screaming and hollering. And she ran outside. And there she was, right on the ground, just red blood, just nothing but blood.
<v Curtis Jefferson>有人敲门。她告诉祖母，她说，芭芭拉倒在地上。我只知道祖母在大叫大嚷。她跑了出去。她就在那里，就在地上，只有红色的血，除了血什么都没有。

00:39:53.640 --> 00:40:11.410
<v Curtis Jefferson>Just looking at it her, I'm five years old, like, what's wrong with Mommy, Grandma? What's wrong with her? My grandma's crying and screaming. She didn't even say nothing. She just keep telling me, go in the house, go in the house, go in the house.
<v Curtis Jefferson>看着她，我当时5岁，像是，妈妈怎么了，祖母？她怎么了？祖母在哭喊。她甚至没说什么。她只是不停地告诉我，进屋去，进屋去，进屋去。

00:40:11.410 --> 00:40:23.020
<v Curtis Jefferson>I was angry a lot, every day, every day, because I thought about my mom, every day, every day, every day. I still think about her every day.
<v Curtis Jefferson>我每天都很生气，每天，因为我每天、每天、每天都想到我妈妈。我仍然每天想她。

00:40:23.020 --> 00:40:33.160
<v Brandon Caro>From that point on, it was very difficult for me to sleep, to focus. I didn't realize how much those things really had made an impact on me, but they did.
<v Brandon Caro>从那时起，我很难睡觉、集中精力。我没意识到这些事情对我产生了多大的影响，但确实有。

00:40:33.160 --> 00:40:46.510
<v Curtis Jefferson>I seen a lot of people get shot. I seen people get shot by cops. And I seen best friends shoot each other. I seen all types of crazy stuff. I don't know.
<v Curtis Jefferson>我看到很多人被枪杀。我看到人被警察枪杀。我看到最好的朋友互相射击。我看到了各种疯狂的事情。我不知道。

00:40:46.510 --> 00:41:09.900
<v Brandon Caro>There was a convoy that went up to Naray, in which there was a sniper attack that killed my old sergeant and his sergeant. There was the rocket-propelled grenade attack on a tent, inside our FOB, followed up by machine-gun fire.
<v Brandon Caro>有一个车队去了纳雷，那里发生了狙击袭击，打死了我的老士官和他的士官。前沿作战基地内发生了一起针对帐篷的火箭助推榴弹袭击，随后是机枪扫射。

00:41:09.900 --> 00:41:28.760
<v Curtis Jefferson>[SIGHS] I could say I had seen a dozen. And for me to not even be like no cop or no doctor or nothing, that's a whole lot to see somebody killed and dead. That's a whole lot.
<v Curtis Jefferson>（叹气）我可以说我见过一打。对我来说，哪怕不是警察或医生，看到有人被杀、死亡，那也是非常多的。那真的非常多。

00:41:28.760 --> 00:41:33.800
<v Alex Kotlowitz>This brings us to the third parallel, they buried their feelings.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>这就引出了第三个平行点，他们埋葬了自己的感情。

00:41:33.800 --> 00:42:00.360
<v Curtis Jefferson>Well, after my mom got shot, I didn't get no counseling. I didn't get no counselor at all. And my aunt asked me, too. My aunt gave me a decision. And she asked me one day. I had to have been, like, 12. She was like, do you want to go to counseling? And I told her, no, for what? I don't need to talk to nobody. I was so much in denial. I think I can handle everything by myself, but I really couldn't.
<v Curtis Jefferson>嗯，我妈妈被枪杀后，我没得到任何咨询。我根本没找过咨询师。我姑姑也问过我。我姑姑给了我一个决定。有一天她问我。我当时一定有12岁了。她说，你想去咨询吗？我告诉她，不，为了什么？我不想跟任何人说话。我当时太否认了。我想我可以独自处理一切，但我真的不行。

00:42:00.360 --> 00:42:38.330
<v Brandon Caro>I think at that point, I was probably still in denial. I knew that I was toting around a lot of emotional baggage. As much as I wanted to talk to people about how I really felt, I also didn't want to talk about it at all. The more I'd give it air, the more real it would be. And I didn't want it to be real. I just wanted it to be over.
<v Brandon Caro>我觉得在那一点上，我可能还在否认。我知道我背负着很多情感包袱。尽管我想跟人谈论我的真实感受，但我根本不想谈论它。我越让它透气，它就越真实。而我不希望它真实。我只想让它结束。

00:42:38.330 --> 00:42:55.380
<v Curtis Jefferson>I can't show no weakness because, you know what I mean? My homies need me out there. And I've been doing that for years. It's to the point, I started getting adapted to it, like, [BLEEP]. But at the same time, I was still scared. Don't nobody want to die like that.
<v Curtis Jefferson>我不能表现出任何软弱，因为，你懂我的意思吗？我的兄弟们需要我在外面。我这样做很多年了。到了这种程度，我已经开始适应它了，比如，（哔）。但与此同时，我仍然害怕。没人想那样死。

00:42:55.380 --> 00:43:00.700
<v Alex Kotlowitz>The fourth shared experience-- raw, unfiltered rage.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>第四个共同经历——原始的、未经过滤的愤怒。

00:43:00.700 --> 00:43:19.810
<v Curtis Jefferson>I was definitely looking for revenge for my mom. When I was about 10 years old, I told my aunt I was going to look for the people that killed my mom. And I was going to go back down to the projects, and I was going to kill them. I always thought like that.
<v Curtis Jefferson>我绝对在为我妈妈报仇。我大约10岁的时候，告诉姑姑我要找杀害我妈妈的人。我要回公屋去，我要杀了他们。我总是那么想。

00:43:19.810 --> 00:43:50.360
<v Brandon Caro>I mean, the way I felt about the Afghans, I began to just hate that whole culture, entirely. I hated them. There was one time, we were driving on an extremely, extremely dangerous road. And we had come very close to falling off the cliff, which would have killed us.
<v Brandon Caro>我是说，我对阿富汗人的感觉，我开始憎恨整个文化。我恨他们。有一次，我们在一条极其危险的路上开车。我们差点从悬崖上掉下去，那会杀了我们。

00:43:50.360 --> 00:44:19.760
<v Brandon Caro>And the truck to our front was a truck filled with Afghan soldiers. And they were pointing and laughing at us for almost falling off. And in that moment, more than any other moment, I wanted to open up on them and kill every one of them. I've realized how much anger and resentment I have, and how dangerous that is.
<v Brandon Caro>我们前面的卡车是一辆装满阿富汗士兵的卡车。他们指着我们笑，因为我们差点掉下去。那一刻，比任何其他时刻，我都想开火，杀死他们每一个人。我意识到我有多少愤怒和怨恨，那是多么危险。

00:44:19.760 --> 00:44:41.250
<v Curtis Jefferson>Man, I shot my sister's boyfriend. I mean, he-- I don't know, I think I had one of them moments. It had to have been, because it just took one word, and it just set me off for no reason, just thinking about everything. And I just shot him.
<v Curtis Jefferson>伙计，我枪击了我姐姐的男朋友。我是说，他……我不知道，我想我当时脑子抽了。一定是的，因为只用了一个词，没有任何理由就让我爆发了，我想到了一切。然后我就开枪打了他。

00:44:41.250 --> 00:44:57.060
<v Curtis Jefferson>I felt some type of way. I made the situation deeper, and it wasn't. You know what I mean? It wasn't even that deep. But I've been holding a lot of stuff in, because he was always trying to discipline me. That's what it was, I didn't like about it, how he always tried to discipline me.
<v Curtis Jefferson>我感觉到了某种方式。我让情况变得更严重了，其实不然。你懂我意思吗？那甚至没那么严重。但我一直憋着很多东西，因为他总是试图管教我。就是那样，我不喜欢它，他总是试图管教我。

00:44:57.060 --> 00:45:19.950
<v Curtis Jefferson>All my sisters are way older than me, they're 40, pushing 50. See what I'm saying? I'm the youngest. And I'm the only boy. So her boyfriends, they're just, oh, you need to stop getting the streets. But it's just how he was saying it. Don't raise your voice at me. He's talking about, yeah, I'm going to take you in the street and fight you and all this. You're going to learn your lesson.
<v Curtis Jefferson>我所有的姐姐都比我大得多，她们40岁，快50了。明白我说的吗？我是家里最小的。我是唯一的男孩。所以她的男朋友们，他们只是，噢，你得停止混街头了。但这只是他说的方式。别对我大声喊叫。他在说，是啊，我要带你到街上跟你打架，还有所有这一切。你会得到教训的。

00:45:19.950 --> 00:45:43.230
<v Curtis Jefferson>It just was the part where he was like, yeah, I'm going to knock you the [BLEEP] out. And that just went in my head, like, what? First thing I do is grab my gun and come around the corner. And he was outside, too. And I'm thinking the whole time, I hope he ain't really outside. So he thinks I'm going to fight him. I'm not fighting this man. I'm going to kill him.
<v Curtis Jefferson>只是在他那样说的那一部分，是啊，我要把你这（哔）家伙打趴下。那句话就钻进我脑子里，像是，什么？我做的第一件事就是抓起我的枪，走到拐角处。他也在外面。我一直在想，我希望他不是真的在外面。所以他以为我要去跟他打架。我才不跟他打。我要杀了他。

00:45:43.230 --> 00:46:11.010
<v Curtis Jefferson>I didn't even give him a chance to put his hands up. As soon as I got around there, he was just all in my face, and just backed up and shot him, shot him in the stomach. I thought I was going to kill him. I thought he was dead, though. I guess everything worked out in its ways. He's ain't a person to tell-- he ain't tell. He still survived, so I don't know.
<v Curtis Jefferson>我甚至没给他举手的机会。我一过去，他就在我脸上，我就后退并开枪打中了他的肚子。我以为我要杀了他。我以为他死了，真的。我猜一切都按各自的方式发展了。他不是那种会告诉……他没告发。他还活着，所以我不知道。

00:46:11.010 --> 00:46:22.370
<v Alex Kotlowitz>Here's something I didn't expect with either of them. The toughest times weren't when they were on the battlefield or in the streets. It was when things were quiet. That's when they struggled the most.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>这是我没料到他们两人会有的情况。最艰难的时候不是他们在战场上或街头时。而是安静的时候。那是他们最挣扎的时候。

00:46:22.370 --> 00:46:45.130
<v Brandon Caro>The worst times, for me, were the times where we weren't out on patrol, because that's when I was alone with my thoughts. And I would try to clear my head. It was impossible. These thoughts would just appear. I would worry. I thought about what it would be like to be shot.
<v Brandon Caro>对我来说，最糟糕的时候是我们没有巡逻的时候，因为那时我独自面对自己的思绪。我会试图理清头绪。这不可能。这些念头就会出现。我会担心。我想象着被枪杀会是什么感觉。

00:46:45.130 --> 00:46:58.770
<v Curtis Jefferson>I might daydream, just look at my ceiling or something, watch TV, like, damn, just imagine bullets going, ripping through my body. You know, mother-[BLEEP] just come out with a gun, just start shooting everything. And I'm getting hit. Everybody getting hit.
<v Curtis Jefferson>我可能会做白日梦，盯着天花板或者什么的，看电视，比如，该死，想象着子弹穿过我的身体。你知道，那个混蛋拿着枪出来，开始扫射一切。然后我中弹了。每个人都中弹了。

00:46:58.770 --> 00:47:19.710
<v Brandon Caro>I would think what it would feel like to be blown up in an IED, to be pinned down, and have the vehicle set ablaze, and to burn alive, to be trapped in the truck because the doors were too heavy to cut through or to pull open.
<v Brandon Caro>我会想被路边炸弹炸死、被困住、车辆着火、活活烧死是什么感觉，因为车门太重无法切开或拉开而困在卡车里。

00:47:19.710 --> 00:47:37.900
<v Curtis Jefferson>Seeing it like this, seeing it with my own eyes, just look, seeing blood, and me just falling to the ground, and is too late for the ambulance? Would I still be alive when the ambulance come get me? Or that crazy kind of stuff, like, when you sit and think about that stuff, that kind of stuff drives you crazy.
<v Curtis Jefferson>亲眼看到它，用自己的眼睛看，就是看，看着血，我倒在地上，救护车来不及了吗？救护车来时我还会活着吗？或者那种疯狂的事情，比如，当你坐下来思考那些事情时，那种事情会让你发疯。

00:47:37.900 --> 00:48:06.250
<v Alex Kotlowitz>For both, reality eventually caught up to their fears. In the spring of 2006, Curtis told us he got caught in a shootout with rival drug dealers. The first bullet, to his back, spun him around. He got hit four more times, in the stomach and in the arm. As a result, he walks doubled over, like an old man. Because a permanent nerve damage, he falls a lot. And when he's under stress or eats the wrong food, he has bleeding from his intestines.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>对两人来说，现实最终追上了他们的恐惧。2006年春天，柯蒂斯告诉我们，他卷入了一场与敌对毒贩的枪战。第一颗子弹击中他的后背，让他转了身。他又中弹四次，在胃部和手臂。结果，他弯着腰走路，像个老人。因为永久的神经损伤，他经常摔倒。当他有压力或吃了错误的食物时，他的肠道会出血。

00:48:06.250 --> 00:48:26.020
<v Alex Kotlowitz>Brandon, too, was hurt in a rollover when his Humvee fell off a small cliff. Brandon had been manning the turret, and fortunately, another soldier pulled him in just as the vehicle rolled. He suffered three fractures of his vertebrae and two herniated disks, and the pain kept him up nights, even after he left Afghanistan.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>布兰登在悍马车从悬崖坠落时也受了伤。布兰登当时在炮塔操纵位置，幸运的是，车辆翻滚时另一名士兵把他拉了进来。他的椎骨发生三处骨折，两处椎间盘突出，疼痛让他即使在离开阿富汗后也彻夜难眠。

00:48:26.020 --> 00:48:34.360
<v Alex Kotlowitz>Both men were on a lot of medications after their injuries. And our sixth similarity may not be so surprising-- they self-medicated.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>两人受伤后都服用了很多药物，我们的第六个相似之处可能并不令人惊讶——他们自我药疗。

00:48:34.360 --> 00:48:40.000
<v Brandon Caro>I had started to drink again. And on the weekends, I would drink heavily.
<v Brandon Caro>我开始重新喝酒。周末，我会大量饮酒。

00:48:40.000 --> 00:48:45.620
<v Curtis Jefferson>It makes my body just feel a little better, like just a little basic, Mary Jane, that's all.
<v Curtis Jefferson>这让我的身体感觉好一点，就像一些基础的“玛丽·简”（大麻），就是这样。

00:48:45.620 --> 00:48:57.610
<v Brandon Caro>Usually a Crown and Coke was my drink of choice. And I would probably drink at least 5, up to 8, 9, 10 in the night.
<v Brandon Caro>通常“皇冠和可乐”是我选择的饮料。我晚上可能会喝至少5杯，多达8、9、10杯。

00:48:57.610 --> 00:49:01.720
<v Curtis Jefferson>But sometimes, it do give me the opposite effect.
<v Curtis Jefferson>但有时，它确实会产生相反的效果。

00:49:01.720 --> 00:49:07.510
<v Brandon Caro>When I was very, very drunk, I would start to cry because of how upset I was.
<v Brandon Caro>当我喝得非常非常醉时，我会因为自己有多难过而开始哭。

00:49:07.510 --> 00:49:25.660
<v Curtis Jefferson>Because, you know, when I smoke, it put me on a mellow. Then, you know, you just start thinking. Then that's when all the thoughts just come, like, oh, what the hell they just come from? And I'd be like, oh, some thoughts I think sometimes, I don't want to think about.
<v Curtis Jefferson>因为，你知道，当我抽烟时，它会让我进入一种平和的状态。然后，你知道，你开始思考。然后这就是所有思绪涌现的时候，比如，噢，它们到底从哪里来的？我会像，噢，有些念头我想的时候，我不想去想。

00:49:25.660 --> 00:49:39.170
<v Alex Kotlowitz>Echo number seven, they lashed out at friends and family. Curtis had night terrors, and would wake in the middle of the night thinking his girlfriend was someone who was trying to shoot him. He'd push her. He'd hit her in the head. He'd call her names.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>第七个共鸣，他们对朋友和家人发火。柯蒂斯有夜惊，他会半夜醒来，以为他的女朋友是试图枪杀他的人。他会推她。他会打她的头。他会骂她。

00:49:39.170 --> 00:49:50.660
<v Curtis Jefferson>I just was like, dang, I know I'm hurting this girl. I'm really putting my hands on her, like a man, putting marks on her arms. And I said, I'm losing it. And I was like, shh.
<v Curtis Jefferson>我只是像，该死，我知道我在伤害这个女孩。我真的像个男人一样对她动手，在她的手臂上留下痕迹。我说，我正在失去理智。我就像，嘘。

00:49:50.660 --> 00:50:07.200
<v Alex Kotlowitz>Brandon had a difficult relationship with a girlfriend, too. They'd get into huge arguments and she'd get scared. One time, Brandon got so agitated, a friend intervened and tried to calm him down. Brandon punched him. His girlfriend locked herself in her room and called the police.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>布兰登和女朋友的关系也很艰难。他们会发生巨大的争吵，她会感到害怕。有一次，布兰登变得如此焦躁，一个朋友介入并试图让他平静下来。布兰登打了他。他的女朋友把自己锁在房间里报了警。

00:50:07.200 --> 00:50:29.580
<v Brandon Caro>Police came. And I was inside. And the police rang the doorbell. And I opened it up. And it was two cops, a guy and a girl cop. And I asked the guy, is your weapon loaded? He said, why would you ask me something like that? I said, because I want you to shoot me in the head.
<v Brandon Caro>警察来了。我在里面。警察按了门铃。我打开门。是两个警察，一个男警和一个女警。我问那个男警，你的枪上膛了吗？他说，你为什么要问我那个？我说，因为我想让你对着我的头开枪。

00:50:29.580 --> 00:51:02.170
<v Curtis Jefferson>I was just sitting in my bedroom. And I had my gun under my bed, in a sneaker box. I had a 40 Mag, big, old cowboy gun. And that's when my grandma caught the gun in my mouth. Yeah, she just opened the door to make sure I was cool. It just was one of them days. She opened the door, and gun in my mouth, you know what I mean?
<v Curtis Jefferson>我当时就坐在卧室里。我把枪放在床底下的鞋盒里。我有一把40口径的马格南，一把很大、很旧的牛仔枪。就在那时，祖母撞见我把枪塞进嘴里。是的，她只是打开门确保我没事。就是那样的日子之一。她打开门，我嘴里叼着枪，你懂我的意思吗？

00:51:02.170 --> 00:51:27.220
<v Curtis Jefferson>She didn't know what to do. She didn't know to come closer or stay back. She didn't know what to do. So she looking at me, start whooping and crying. And she didn't want to come next to me. And so she's just talking to me the whole time, like, come on, baby, it's all right, I love you. You're my grandson. You know, your mom ain't leave you out here on the Earth for this. You blessed. You just got shot five times. Why are you?
<v Curtis Jefferson>她不知道该做什么。她不知道该走近还是后退。她不知道该做什么。所以她看着我，开始哀号和哭泣。她不想靠近我。所以她一直在跟我说话，比如，来吧，宝贝，没事的，我爱你。你是我的孙子。你知道，你妈妈把你留在地球上不是为了这个。你是被祝福的。你才刚中了五枪。你为什么？

00:51:27.220 --> 00:51:55.580
<v Curtis Jefferson>It's going to be all right. We're going to get some help for you and everything. And I just took it out, decocked it, put it back in the box, and just sat there. And called my homie up, and bring some weed around the corner, and got high. And that's how that went.
<v Curtis Jefferson>一切都会好起来的。我们会为你找点帮助之类的。我只是把它拿出来，放下击锤，放回盒子里，坐在那里。打电话给我的兄弟，带点大麻过来，我就吸嗨了。事情就是这样发展的。

00:51:55.580 --> 00:52:22.180
<v Alex Kotlowitz>Both Brandon and Curtis have gotten help. Brandon's in AA and went through a writing program for veterans at NYU. Curtis is receiving counseling through the program at Drexel that offers help to guys coming off the street. But finally, it's here where their stories diverge. Brandon's tour ended, and he's now thousands of miles from the dangers of Afghanistan. Curtis still lives in his old neighborhood, where the danger's ongoing.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>布兰登和柯蒂斯都得到了帮助。布兰登在参加戒酒互助会，并参加了纽约大学为退伍军人开设的写作项目。柯蒂斯正在通过德雷塞尔大学的项目接受咨询，该项目为脱离街头的人提供帮助。但最终，他们的故事在这里分道扬镳。布兰登的服役期结束，现在他离阿富汗的危险有数千英里之远。柯蒂斯依然住在他的老社区，那里的危险还在继续。

00:52:22.180 --> 00:52:40.230
<v Curtis Jefferson>When I get more money, I'm definitely moving. I'm going to move. That's a priority I'm working on right now. I'm out. I'm going to live somewhere comfortable, that I know I can walk in the neighborhood, I could sit out there on my step all day, all night, if I want to.
<v Curtis Jefferson>当我赚到更多钱时，我绝对会搬家。我会搬走。这是我目前正在努力的优先事项。我要离开。我要住在一个舒适的地方，我知道我可以走在社区里，如果我想的话，我可以整天整夜坐在台阶上。

00:52:40.230 --> 00:52:46.800
<v Alex Kotlowitz>Not long ago, a guy was shot and killed down the block from where he lives. And Curtis happened to see the body on the ground.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>不久前，一个家伙在他住的街区被枪杀了。柯蒂斯恰好看到地上的尸体。

00:52:46.800 --> 00:53:05.420
<v Curtis Jefferson>And I just was thinking about a lot of the-- I was paused. I just was like, yo, when he shot, like, damn, that boy was on the ground. And that was me. It's just, I'm living. Like, he died. And I'm like, damn. And it just felt like I felt all them bullets all over again.
<v Curtis Jefferson>我只是在想很多……我暂停了。我只是像，哟，当他被射中时，就像，该死，那个男孩在地上。那是我。我活着。而他死了。我像是，该死。感觉就像我再次感受到了所有那些子弹。

00:53:05.420 --> 00:53:22.380
<v Alex Kotlowitz>Curtis worries that because of the way he walks, perpetually bent over, he looks weak and that people will target him, rob him, beat him up, shoot him, take revenge. I wonder, for someone like Curtis, if it's really post-traumatic stress, since really, there's nothing post about it.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>柯蒂斯担心因为他走路的方式，总是弯着腰，他看起来很弱，人们会盯上他、抢劫他、殴打他、射击他、进行报复。我不知道，对于柯蒂斯这样的人来说，这是否真的是创伤后应激障碍，因为实际上，并没有什么“后”的事情。

00:53:22.380 --> 00:53:36.420
<v Alex Kotlowitz>Brandon, meanwhile, says he doesn't talk to his friends or family about his anger and his nightmares, which he still has regularly. He tried counseling, but didn't like it. So he sought out other veterans who understand what he's been through.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>与此同时，布兰登说他不会跟朋友或家人谈论他的愤怒和噩梦，尽管他仍然经常做。他尝试过咨询，但没用。所以他寻找其他理解他经历的退伍军人。

00:53:36.420 --> 00:53:54.790
<v Alex Kotlowitz>And Curtis now attends group counseling with others who, like himself, were once running the streets. That's the final echo. They've both come to realize that they're not alone.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>而柯蒂斯现在和那些像他一样曾经在街头混过的人一起参加团体咨询。这是最后一个共鸣。他们都意识到他们并不孤单。

00:53:54.790 --> 00:53:56.290
<v Fred Armisen>Alex Kotlowitz.
<v Fred Armisen>亚历克斯·科特洛维茨。

00:53:56.290 --> 00:54:30.750
<v Ira Glass>His books and documentaries include, most recently, the film The Interrupters, which he wrote and co-produced, which is at iTunes, Netflix, Amazon, and on DVD.
<v Ira Glass>他的书和纪录片包括最近的电影《干扰者》，他编写并共同制作，在iTunes、Netflix、亚马逊和DVD上有售。

00:54:30.750 --> 00:54:31.970
<v Ira Glass>OK, so should we do the credits?
<v Ira Glass>好的，那我们该录制作名单了吗？

00:54:31.970 --> 00:54:36.800
<v Fred Armisen>Yeah, let's do it. Are you ready? God, you do this whole thing at the end of every show?
<v Fred Armisen>是的，让我们做吧。你准备好了吗？天呐，你每一集节目的结尾都做这个？

00:54:36.800 --> 00:54:38.580
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. OK. Ready?
<v Ira Glass>是的。好的。准备好了吗？

00:54:38.580 --> 00:54:44.170
<v Fred Armisen>You've got to hire someone to do that. (IMITATING IRA GLASS) Well, our program was produced today by Miki Meek and myself--
<v Fred Armisen>你得雇个人来做那个。（模仿艾拉·格拉斯）嗯，我们的节目今天由米基·米克和我制作——

00:54:44.170 --> 00:54:49.000
<v Ira Glass>With Alex Blumberg, Ben Calhoun, Sarah Koenig, Jonathan Menjivar, Brian Reed, Robyn Semien, Alissa Shipp, and Nancy Updike.
<v Ira Glass>还有亚历克斯·布隆伯格、本·卡尔霍恩、萨拉·科尼格、乔纳森·门吉瓦尔、布莱恩·里德、罗宾·塞米恩、阿丽莎·希普和南希·厄普代克。

00:54:49.000 --> 00:54:50.820
<v Fred Armisen>(IMITATING IRA GLASS) Our senior producer's Julie Snyder.
<v Fred Armisen>（模仿艾拉·格拉斯）我们的资深制作人是朱莉·斯奈德。

00:54:50.820 --> 00:54:52.830
<v Ira Glass>Production help from Tarek Fouda and Phia Bennin.
<v Ira Glass>制作协助来自塔雷克·福达和菲亚·本宁。

00:54:52.830 --> 00:54:54.660
<v Fred Armisen>(IMITATING IRA GLASS) Seth Lind is our operations director.
<v Fred Armisen>（模仿艾拉·格拉斯）塞斯·林德是我们的运营总监。

00:54:54.660 --> 00:54:56.200
<v Ira Glass>Emily Condon's our production manager.
<v Ira Glass>艾米丽·康登是我们的制作经理。

00:54:56.200 --> 00:54:58.290
